// The sdf package: signed distance fields, shapes as functions from a point
// to its distance from the surface (negative inside). A program reaches it
// with 'import sdf', and gets everything in sdfcore, re-exported (so
// 'import sdf use *' and 'sdf.sphere' reach it as they always have), and
// this package's own CPU side:
//
// - From sdfcore, the GPU-safe half: the primitives, the operators on
//   distances and on the point, hg_sdf's fillets, the Curve and Repeated
//   traits with 'repeatAlong', 'Arc' and 'repeatAlongArc', the Shape trait
//   with 'gradient' and 'normal', and detail from noise. sdfcore's root,
//   sdfcore.cone, lists them.
// - Curves that hold lists (pathcurve.cone): 'PathCurve' (a sculpt.Path),
//   and 'CurveCells', 'curveCells' and 'repeatAlongCells', the same union
//   over any curve's cells found once.
// - The horn (horn.cone): 'Horn', a ribbed, tapering, curling horn, the
//   union of its cells along an Arc. (Its code is GPU-safe; it stays here
//   while whether it belongs in the library at all is open.)
// - 'FnShape' (fnshape.cone), a function reference as a Shape.
// - Meshing (mesher.cone): 'surfaceNet', a shape made into a manifold
//   'SurfaceNet' of quads on the CPU (surface nets, one vertex per piece of
//   surface in a cell, gradient normals, blocks far from the surface
//   skipped), its 'toMesh' (a mesh.Mesh) and 'toPolyMesh'; 'netLevels', a
//   chain of levels of detail; 'meshHash', a hash of a mesh's bits.
//
// Numbers are f32, points geomath's Vec3. A shape is written as code:
//
//   fn scene(p Vec3) f32 {
//     imm body = sphere(p, 1.);
//     imm hole = cylinder(rotate(p, turn), 2., 0.4);
//     smoothSubtract(body, hole, 0.1);
//   }
//
// EXACT AND BOUNDED. A distance is exact when it is the Euclidean distance
// to the surface, a bound when it never exceeds it (a sphere tracer stays
// safe, and a mesher finds the same surface). Each function's comment says
// which it gives; the tests sample the gradient's length (at most 1 for
// either) for those that claim one.
//
// CPU ONLY. What is here allocates or holds lists (the mesher, PathCurve,
// CurveCells), or holds a function reference (FnShape): GPU code imports
// sdfcore instead. The horn is the exception, kept here though it is not.
//
// THE REPRESENTATION IS NOT DECIDED. Shapes are composed as functions here,
// on the CPU and, the same functions in the same order, in sdf.slang. Every
// function is one node of an operator graph (a primitive with its sizes, an
// operator with its constants, a domain operator on the point), so a data
// tree that can be sent and evaluated can be built over these later, if
// that is what crosses the network.
//
// sdf.slang holds the same functions for shaders, sdfcore's and the horn's,
// under the same names (all but PathCurve; the constructors are functions
// there, 'makeArc', 'makeArcCells', 'makeHorn'; the traits are interfaces
// and the generic functions Slang generics over them). The two are kept in
// step by hand; tests/parity.cone checks them against each other on a GPU.
// Those without square roots, division or trigonometry agree bit for bit,
// the rest to within 1.5e-5; the README has what was measured.
//
// Its files are one module, and only this one, the root, imports:
//
//   scalar.cone     private helpers, a copy of those sdfcore keeps private
//   pathcurve.cone  PathCurve, CurveCells, curveCells, repeatAlongCells
//   horn.cone       Horn
//   fnshape.cone    FnShape
//   mesher.cone     SurfaceNet, surfaceNet, netLevels, meshHash
//
// Meshing lives here, not in mesh: it needs a Shape, and mesh must not
// depend on sdf (sdf already depends on mesh, through sculpt). What it
// makes is mesh's Mesh and PolyMesh.

mod sdf;

import libc;
import geomath use *;
import collections use *;
import sculpt;
import noise;
import mesh;
import sdfcore pub use *;
